      <h1 class="article__title">
        How We Source and Verify Our Visa Data
      </h1>

      <p class="article__meta">September 10, 2026 · 4 min read</p>

      <p class="article__audience">
        <strong>This post is for you if:</strong> you want to know where
        our stay-limit rules come from and why you can rely on them for
        planning real travel.
        <br/>
        <br/>
        <strong>It's probably not necessary if:</strong> you're only
        looking for today's calculation and don't need to see the sourcing
        behind it.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="article__body">
      <p>
        Every stay-limit calculation on EuroVisaCalculator traces back to a
        specific government publication. Not a summarized version of one,
        not a third-party aggregator, and not a chatbot's best guess. Here
        is how we source that data and how we keep it accurate as visa
        regimes change.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>We cite official government sources for every rule</h2>

      <p>
        Each destination in EuroVisaCalculator is backed by a dedicated
        data file that references the exact regulation, statutory
        instrument, or official guidance page it comes from. Every passport
        rule in the app carries a citation to that source, so the results
        you see trace back to a specific legal text or official Ministry of Foreign Affairs webpage.
      </p>

      <p>
        For the Schengen Area, two regulations work together. The <a
          href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/399/2025-10-12/eng"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Schengen Borders Code (Regulation (EU) 2016/399)</a
        > sets the conditions of entry for third-country nationals and
        defines the 90-days-in-any-180-day-period limit itself. This is the
        legal basis for the rolling-window calculation at the center of the
        app. Alongside it, <a
          href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A02018R1806-20251230"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Regulation (EU) 2018/1806</a
        >, Annexes I and II, sets out which nationalities require a visa
        and which travel visa-free. That regulation is the source for
        every passport's baseline access category.
      </p>

      <p>
        Where a nationality's access depends on an airport transit visa, we
        cite the Schengen Visa Code <a
          href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2009/810/oj/eng"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >(Regulation (EC) 810/2009) Annex IV common list</a
        >, plus the Visa Code Handbook Annex 7B for the
        additional, member-state-specific requirements that individual
        countries impose beyond the common EU list.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same approach carries through outside Schengen. The UK's
        Standard Visitor route and Electronic Travel Authorisation
        requirements are sourced from GOV.UK and the Immigration Rules'
        Appendix Visitor Visa National List and Appendix ETA National List.
        Ireland's non-visa-required nationality table comes from the Irish
        Naturalisation and Immigration Service. Türkiye's rules, including
        several nationality-specific exceptions to the standard 90/180
        window, are sourced from Türkiye's e-Visa system and its published
        bilateral arrangements. Montenegro, Serbia, Bosnia and Herzegovina,
        Kosovo, North Macedonia, and Albania each cite their own national
        visa legislation. Cyprus, an EU member outside the Schengen
        border-free area, is governed by the same Regulation 2018/1806 as
        Schengen, so its rules cite that regulation directly rather than a
        separate national source.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes information on who is permitted visa-free entry to a jurisdiction is not colocated
        with information on the duration limits afforded to visitors with visa-free eligibility.
        In certain cases, official sources for such stay length limits are unavailable, so we may default
        to using widely-reported information from other sources. In such cases, we make it very clear when
        no official source is available for citation.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Sources are checked regularly</h2>

      <p>
        A link alone doesn't tell you whether the underlying rule is still
        accurate. Alongside every citation, we track the date each source
        was last confirmed current. When a rule changes, that date is what tells us a
        citation needs a second look.
      </p>

      <p>
        We also keep a stable "parent" policy-page link alongside every
        direct document citation, since government sites reorganize and
        PDFs move. If a direct document link ever breaks, we can fall back
        to the parent page rather than lose the reference entirely.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>We watch for regime changes</h2>

      <p>
        Visa regimes change. A country adjusts a reciprocal exemption, the
        EU updates Annex I or Annex II, a nationality is added to an
        airport transit list, or ETIAS shifts from planned to mandatory. We
        revisit our sources on an ongoing basis, and when a change affects
        an existing rule, we update it and republish.
      </p>

      <p>
        Whether automated or manually, all sources are regularly confirmed to be accurate,
        and all candidates for change are reported and eventually approved by a human.
        While our checks are frequent, policy changes in a nation's visa regime 
        can occur without warning. EuroVisaCalculator is inherently reactive to
        these changes and can sometimes be out of date, if a new accuracy check
        has not yet been issued. For this reason, we recommend users follow our citations
        links to confirm the official documentation themselves.
      </p>

      <p>
        We've written about the mechanics of the underlying rules in more
        detail elsewhere, and comparing those posts against the government
        texts we cite is one way to check our work yourself:
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/blog/the-schengen-90-180-rolling-window"
          >The Schengen 90/180 Rolling Window, Explained Visually</a
        > — how the rolling window itself works, walked through five
        scenarios.
        <br/>
        <a href="/blog/does-your-entry-day-count-as-a-schengen-day"
          >Does Your Entry Day Count as a Schengen Day?</a
        > — why border days count as full days under the official rule.
        <br/>
        <a href="/blog/when-does-schengen-reset"
          >When Does Your Schengen Allowance Reset?</a
        > — why the 180-day window doesn't reset like a calendar.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Why this matters more than a confident-sounding answer</h2>

      <p>
        We've also written about <a href="/blog/why-schengen-calculators-give-different-results"
          >why Schengen calculators give different results</a
        >. A plausible number is not the same as a verified one, and a
        calculator that can't show you where its underlying rule comes from
        can't be checked against anything. That is also why an AI chatbot
        is not a substitute for this: it can generate a confident answer,
        but it cannot cite the regulation it came from.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>If something looks off</h2>

      <p>
        If you ever run your dates and get a result that doesn't match what
        you find in the source we've cited, or a rule that seems out of
        date, cross-check it against that source, or against the <a
          href="https://ec.europa.eu/assets/home/visa-calculator/calculator.htm"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >official EU calculator</a
        >. If they ever disagree, we want to know.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a href="/app">Run your dates through EuroVisaCalculator →</a>
      </p>
    </div>

